example.paragraph:
Let $\mathbf{v}_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}$, $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      5
      \end{bmatrix}$, $\mathbf{v}_{3} = \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      3
      \end{bmatrix}$, $\mathbf{v}_{4} = \begin{bmatrix}
      {- 2} \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}$, and $\text{y} = \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}$. If possible, write $\textbf{y}$ as an affine combination of $\textbf{v}_{1}$, $\textbf{v}_{2}$, $\textbf{v}_{3}$ and $\textbf{v}_{4}$.﻿

Find the $B\text{-coordinates}$ of $\textbf{p}_{1}$ and $\textbf{p}_{2}$. These two calculations can be combined by row reducing the matrix $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{lllll}
      \mathbf{b}_{1} & \mathbf{b}_{2} & \mathbf{b}_{3} & \mathbf{p}_{1} & \mathbf{p}_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, with two augmented columns:

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
      {(1 - t)\mathbf{s}_{1} + t\mathbf{s}_{2}} & = & {(1 - t)\left( \mathbf{u}_{1} + \mathbf{p} \right) + t\left( \mathbf{u}_{2} + \mathbf{p} \right)} \\
       & = & {(1 - t)\mathbf{u}_{1} + (1 - t)\mathbf{p} + t\mathbf{u}_{2} + t\mathbf{p}} \\
       & = & {(1 - t)\mathbf{u}_{1} + t\mathbf{u}_{2} + \mathbf{p}}
      \end{matrix}$$

example.paragraph:
Suppose that the solutions of an equation $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ are all of the form $\textbf{x} = x_{3}\textbf{u} + \textbf{p}$, where $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 3} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\mathbf{p} = \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \\
      0 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{bmatrix}\,$, Recall from Section 1.5 that this set is parallel to the solution set of $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{0}$, which consists of all points of the form $x_{3}\textbf{u}$. Find points $\textbf{v}_{1}$ and $\textbf{v}_{2}$ such that the solution set of $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ is $\text{aff}\left\{ \textbf{v}_{1},\textbf{v}_{2} \right\}$.﻿

note.paragraph:
Plot the points $\mathbf{v}_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      0
      \end{bmatrix}$, $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{v}_{3} = \begin{bmatrix}
      3 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}$, and $\mathbf{p} = \begin{bmatrix}
      4 \\
      3
      \end{bmatrix}$ on graph paper, and explain why $\textbf{p}$ must be an affine combination of $\textbf{v}_{1}$, $\textbf{v}_{2}$, and $\textbf{v}_{3}$. Then find the affine combination for $\textbf{p}$. [Hint: What is the dimension of $\text{aff}\{\textbf{v}_{1}\text{,}$ $\textbf{v}_{2}\text{,}$ $\left. \textbf{v}_{3} \right\}$?]﻿

