exercise_group.instructions:
In Exercises 5 and 6, let $\textbf{b}_{1} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      1 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}\,$ ,﻿ $\textbf{b}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      0 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$ ,﻿ $\textbf{b}_{3} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 5} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$ , and $S = \left\{ \textbf{b}_{1},\textbf{b}_{2},\textbf{b}_{3} \right\}$﻿. Note that $S$ is an orthogonal basis for $\text{ℝ}^{3}$﻿. Write each of the given points as an affine combination of the points in the set $S$﻿, if possible. [Hint: Use Theorem 5 in Section 6.2 instead of row reduction to find the weights.]

statement.paragraph:
Suppose that the solutions of an equation $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ are all of the form $\textbf{x} = x_{3}\textbf{u} + \textbf{p}$, where $\textbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\textbf{p} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Find points $\textbf{v}_{1}$ and $\textbf{v}_{2}$ such that the solution set of $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ is $\text{aff}\left\{ \mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2} \right\}$.﻿

Suppose that the solutions of an equation $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ are all of the form $\textbf{x} = x_{3}\textbf{u} + \textbf{p}$, where $\textbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      1 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\textbf{p} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 3} \\
      4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,$. Find points $\textbf{v}_{1}$ and $\textbf{v}_{2}$ such that the solution set of $A\textbf{x} = \textbf{b}$ is $\text{aff}\left\{ \mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2} \right\}$.﻿
